I've now recorded three distinct traces for the Kripke `kripke.exe` subject, corresponding to its major build variants. Summary:

**kripke.exe : cpu-mpi-openmp** — default/Sequential+OpenMP build with optional MPI. Wavefront upwind/downwind sweep communication (`MPI_Isend/Irecv` in `ParallelComm.cpp`) plus `MPI_Allreduce` reductions for source-iteration norms (`Core/Comm.h`). Ordered dependency (sweep is a wavefront algorithm per README and `SweepComm.cpp`). No parallel I/O, no checkpoint/restart, no GPU. OpenMP threading confirmed via `#pragma omp parallel` diagnostic in `kripke.cpp`.

**kripke.exe : mpi-cuda** — `ENABLE_CUDA` build, RAJA `cuda_exec`/`CudaKernel` on-node kernels (`Kripke.h`), same MPI sweep/reduction communication pattern, optional GPU-aware MPI path gated behind CHAI (`ENABLE_GPU_AWARE_MPI`).

**kripke.exe : mpi-rocm** — `ENABLE_HIP` build, RAJA `hip_exec` kernels, structurally identical to the CUDA variant (same sweep/reduction/GPU-aware-MPI options), confirmed by HIP host-config files for MI250X/MI300A.

All three share the same core algorithmic shape (Sn transport source-iteration with an asynchronous MPI wavefront sweep across spatial subdomains, group-sets and direction-sets), differing only in the on-node compute backend (CPU/OpenMP vs CUDA vs HIP). No evidence of shared-memory windows, NUMA pinning, huge pages, or parallel I/O/checkpointing was found in any variant.
